Z||000000||fotofrank||08-28-2001||01:26 PM|||| I find it nessesary to replace the water pump on my '84 2.5L engin. What is the easiest way to do this? I have air conditioning(not working at the present).<BR>TIA<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||9
Z||000001||fotofrank||08-28-2001||05:41 PM||||please!! ||||reg||9
Z||000002||theogre||08-28-2001||07:05 PM||||There is no easy way. You'll probably need a special tool to change the pulley. You can gain some room by removing the battery and heat shield.||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||08-28-2001||09:04 PM||||As was said, it isn't easy. I replaced mine and I also have a non-working AC. I reached the 3/8 extensions through holes in the AC mounting bracket to unscrew the bolts holding the water pump manifold onto the block. <P>Before I did the foregoing step, I drained the coolant and removed the hoses from the water pump manifold.<P>Then I removed the water pump-manifold assembly. <P>I got a remanufactured water pump with installed pulley (2 year warranty) from Autozone. It has a metal impeller.<P>If I recall correctly the fitting that the hose connects to also has to come off of the manifold before the water pump/manifold assembly is removed from the block. It is sealed only with RTV silicone sealant so you have to clean it up and reseal it during the installation process.<P>Take the old pump off of the manifold and clean up the manifold. Note that the gasket between the manifold and the block had no silicone sealant on it so when you put it back together, don't use any.<P>Bolt the new pump with the new bolts (they come with the remanufactured pump) to the manifold. Use a small amount of silicone sealant on both sides of the gasket between the pump and manifold.<P>I think that I had to bolt the manifold/pump assembly to the block using a new gasket before installing the fitting that the hose clamps to.<P>Fill system with coolant, put a new alternator / water pump belt on, start the engine, and test for leaks.<P>I used a haynes manual when I changed my water pump. <P>Good Luck.<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 08-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||beachbomb||08-29-2001||08:59 AM||||Hey, i replaced mine a month ago, of course i had the engine out of the car when i did it, it wasn't bad at all, hardest part was finding a replacement with the proper pulley on it(2 groove instead of the universal 3 groove) i found the good 2 groove with already mounted pully at Napa auto parts. cost about $30. ||||reg||4
Z||000005||starfighter007@msn.com||08-29-2001||11:24 AM||||You should go see v-8 Archie as hes only a few miles from you in Barrington Il. he may have 4 cyl.laying around that you can look at and see whats up before you start||||reg||1
